A||N||13||FieroMorg||Bumper change||||6||
Z||000000||FieroMorg||12-09-1999||01:37 PM||||Hi all Fiero lovers!<P>I have an 85 SE and I have found a pair of 86 GT front and rear bumpers to put on.  Do I need anything special to make them fit on or do you just screw them on?  ||||reg||6
Z||000001||RZURCTN||12-09-1999||11:32 PM||||As far as I know, you can just bolt them on.  Someone correct me if I'm wrong!||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||12-09-1999||11:45 PM||||Well, you have to take the old ones off first, that's very important.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||12-10-1999||03:52 AM||||batboy--you don't HAVE to take the old one off. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  If you're really good with fiberglass...<BR>Seriously, though...Sometimes you have to trim the impact strip thing, but that it's not a big deal.  I've been reading about it as that's what I plan to do in the spring.  If you can get the old impact webbing, get it. If not, get a Dremel tool just in case.||||reg||1
Z||000004||JetroGT||12-10-1999||04:48 AM||||You will also have to change your molding and side marker lights.||||reg||14
Z||000005||Wolfhound||12-10-1999||05:21 AM||||The wheelwell liners are not the same. If you have access to the donor car swap them, this is easier than altering the existing ones,which can be done. The rear liner is in two pieces, you only have to replace the rear piece. There were a few different rivit positions that had to be drilled (maybe four) I forget. Minor though!<BR>I recently swapped an 85 SE with 86 GT body with the exception of the fastback. I swapped the front fenders, outer door panels and lower rear quarter as well for the ground effects. Couldn't figure out how to get the upper ground effect pieces off without damaging them. Removing the two and replacing the one took a day and a half. The inner fenders are held on with plastic rivits that have what appears to be a phillips head screw. It will not screw out. pry the screw portion out with a knife and you can reuse them. other plastic rivits I had to replace I found at Lowe,s building supply in the fastner area.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroMorg||12-10-1999||09:01 AM||||Thanks very much for your replies....||||reg||10
Z||000007||Voyd||12-10-1999||10:13 AM||||hmm im gonna do this exact same thing this spring, with the exception that i alread have the front fascia.<BR> when u say change the moldings, do u mean get new moldings or just take them off temporarily? are the moldings glued on or what? <P>i know the marker lights are held there by 2 screws and are easy to take off.<P>and FieroMorg, please let us (well me at least) know how it went and if u have any tips or probs along the way!<p>[This message has been edited by Voyd (edited 12-10-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Voyd||12-10-1999||10:17 AM||||hey Wolfhound, i was wondering also, how do you get the ground effects onto a 85SE? do u have to drill some holes or sumthin? ||||reg||1
Z||000009||88 Fiero||12-10-1999||10:23 AM||||Also...when you do swap the front and rear bumpers on your 85SE to a GT bumper...don't forget to change the honeycomber protector inside...that's the one when you take your bumper off and you see this I think gray or black like honeycomb support..The GT's are different fron the sports coupe and it supports your bumper properly....||||reg||1
Z||000010||Wolfhound||12-10-1999||11:02 AM||||Voyd, The lower ground effect is removed first. there are three black plastic covers you remove that expose I think, four rivits. Another set is under the car in view. The upper portion I couldn't figure out an easy way to remove without damage. Also you have the problem of redrilling the fender, door, and lower quarter. Also they are flat ovals not just holes. So I just swapped all these panels. If you do this everything is in place on the subframe for it to work.<BR>The final appearance is an 85GT. If I run across some inexpensive taillights and quarter windows I may complete the transition. I need all four. More than I want to spend on it at the moment. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||william_thorniii||12-10-1999||08:30 PM||||Only one problem with the posts so far.  Those fasteners that hold in the wheel well liners are (under normal circumstances) easily and non-destructively removed by backing them out like a screw.  While removing mine on the rear (#@%! alternators) only two were permanently damaged.  <P>Hope that saves someone about a $.25.  Every little bit helps!<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000012||Voyd||12-11-1999||09:41 AM||||thanks, but i dont have a donor car, only the 85SE. guess im gonna have to drill huh. but since im going with a mono paint scheme, the ground effects wouldnt make a big diffrence over the regular ones i dont think...i mean they are nice but it seems like a lot of trouble to put on.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Scratchy||12-11-1999||10:12 AM||||Hi Voyd,<P>I read somewhere in another post,if I remember, that you are from montreal (a least from Quebec) well, so am I.  If ever you hear of a good deal in our area on a Fiero,please let me know, I'm looking for one.  Also, do you know of any other owners/forumners in Quebec?<BR>Thanks,<BR>Fred<BR>P.S.  You can email me at BOUF74@hotmail.com||||reg||1
